GS CPU rasterizer: triangles follow the GS rules. Vertex positions are 12.4 fixed point (XYOFFSET with its fraction, which the game uses to draw the odd field half a line lower), a pixel is sampled at its integer coordinate and not at +0.5, and a pixel exactly on an edge belongs to the triangle only for a top or a left edge, so two triangles that share an edge draw it once (the old inclusive epsilon drew shared edges twice, visible where geometry is blended). Colours, depth and texture coordinates are interpolated at the same integer point; a vertex with no weight adds nothing (0 times an infinite S was NaN). Measured against PCSX2 (software renderer, the same eleven city views, native frame buffers from savestates): the mean pixel difference fell from 6 to 12 to 4 to 9; attributes at the pixel middle instead doubled it. Four unit tests assumed sampling at +0.5 or read the swizzled frame buffer as a linear array; they now check the same things at the integer sample point. PS2X_GS_PROBE=x,y[,skip[,count[,seconds]]] logs every write to one frame buffer pixel with the draw state and the pixel before and after.

